1
00:00:00,362 --> 00:00:02,662
Ask “Why does zero pass validation?”

2
00:00:03,466 --> 00:00:06,052
in a fresh chat. Depending on the
session,

3
00:00:06,294 --> 00:00:11,410
Copilot may already receive parts of the
active editor or search the workspace.

4
00:00:11,846 --> 00:00:16,800
Look for the sources cited in its
answer. Even a correct guess is easier

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:19,546
to evaluate once you know where it came
from.

6
00:00:20,041 --> 00:00:22,681
Next we will choose the evidence
deliberately.

7
00:00:24,398 --> 00:00:29,151
Select three things from the training
checkout. The issue tells you what

8
00:00:29,151 --> 00:00:29,766
should happen.

9
00:00:30,306 --> 00:00:32,997
The validator tells you what the program
does now.

10
00:00:33,342 --> 00:00:36,538
The test file tells you what behavior is
already covered.

11
00:00:36,953 --> 00:00:39,781
If you only have the issue, you can
explain the rule.

12
00:00:40,104 --> 00:00:44,264
With the source, you can identify
exactly which comparison breaks it.

13
00:00:45,690 --> 00:00:48,809
Open the validator and the test file in
VS Code.

14
00:00:49,487 --> 00:00:52,379
Use the chat attachment control to add
those files.

15
00:00:52,944 --> 00:00:57,015
Include the synthetic issue text and
inspect the list before sending.

16
00:00:57,362 --> 00:01:00,768
Confirm the paths are from the checkout
you intend to work on.

17
00:01:01,090 --> 00:01:05,870
This lets you and the assistant refer to
the same function and the same tests.

18
00:01:07,819 --> 00:01:11,416
Ask two questions together. “Why does
zero pass,

19
00:01:11,957 --> 00:01:13,968
and which baseline test covers it?”

20
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:19,212
The answer should show that greater than
or equal to zero returns true for zero.

21
00:01:19,533 --> 00:01:24,488
Read the tests for one, five, and
negative one. None runs the zero case.

22
00:01:25,051 --> 00:01:29,443
That gap explains why the existing suite
can be green while the issue remains

23
00:01:29,455 --> 00:01:30,105
open.

24
00:01:31,672 --> 00:01:35,441
Imagine an older attachment shows a
greater-than comparison.

25
00:01:35,878 --> 00:01:39,336
The active checkout still has greater
than or equal to zero.

26
00:01:39,727 --> 00:01:42,291
Open the current file and confirm the
branch,

27
00:01:42,579 --> 00:01:44,499
then remove the stale attachment.

28
00:01:45,016 --> 00:01:47,518
Ask the question again using the current
source.

29
00:01:48,104 --> 00:01:51,400
A one-character difference is enough to
change the answer,

30
00:01:51,847 --> 00:01:54,592
so keep the two versions easy to
distinguish.

31
00:01:56,375 --> 00:02:00,592
Try a follow-up where you provide the
function but leave out the issue.

32
00:02:01,132 --> 00:02:05,345
Ask what the program does and what
evidence would tell you whether that is

33
00:02:05,345 --> 00:02:05,995
correct.

34
00:02:06,387 --> 00:02:10,354
The code answers the first question. The
issue answers the second.

35
00:02:10,929 --> 00:02:15,263
After you provide it, ask the assistant
to compare the two and check that it

36
00:02:15,263 --> 00:02:17,508
keeps both claims tied to their sources.

37
00:02:19,292 --> 00:02:22,897
Run the same question in two fresh
sessions on the same branch.

38
00:02:23,483 --> 00:02:28,319
In the first attach the issue alone. In
the second add the validator and tests.

39
00:02:28,802 --> 00:02:31,744
Compare their claims about the cause and
coverage.

40
00:02:32,308 --> 00:02:37,234
Look for a real source line and a named
test. Write one sentence about which

41
00:02:37,234 --> 00:02:39,608
attachment made each claim checkable.
